          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her aside independently frames the issue as love that she cannot adequately verbalize, so the expression is a clear avowal rather than merely conventional address.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression explicitly invokes and affirms Cordelia’s existing filial bond: she loves Lear “According to my bond” and then identifies that bond with fitting duties of obedience, love, and honour. It does not create a new promise; it positions her as bound by and returning the obligations arising from their father-daughter relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"You have begot me, bred me, lov’d me: I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The occurrence is a controlled, reasoned avowal qualified by the limits of Cordelia’s bond. Although she is distressed, the words are not represented as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.83,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and affirming obligation, the expression has a corrective and boundary-setting function. Cordelia answers Lear’s competitive demand while refusing an unlimited or hyperbolic claim, restricting her declaration to the proper measure of her filial bond.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public division of his kingdom, Lear asks each daughter to say how much she loves him, linking the answer to his bounty. Cordelia refuses competitive hyperbole and gives a deliberately bounded avowal of filial love and duty.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms and invokes an existing filial undertaking—fitting duties of obedience, love, and honour—rather than making a new promise. Its detailed limits remain broad, though she distinguishes these duties from the love and care she expects to owe a future husband. Lear does not accept the declaration as an adequate response to his demand: he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports Cordelia’s present affection through her asides, but it does not by itself establish the future duration or fulfilment of every duty she invokes. Her bond is identified generally as filial duty rather than specified as a complete set of concrete obligations.\"}"
        }
